WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.650
- You're going to learn three ways

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.170
to be seen as the leader in the room

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.000
and we're gonna reverse engineer this.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.640
As we look at these top three
skill sets you'll notice

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.620
if you think about the
best leaders you know,

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.060
this is how they show up.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.770
And once you know what they're doing,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.900
you can improve those same skills.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.030
So let's assume you're
not the official leader

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.350
in the room every time yet.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.540
How can you establish yourself

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.240
as an obvious emerging
leader in that group?

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.420
And I have just one caveat,
before I give you the top three,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.330
I'm assuming that you're
already good and competent

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.420
at your actual individual
job responsibilities.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.630
In no way should we ever
view communication skills

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.230
as a way to hide your
shortcomings or incompetence,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.800
your knowledge, skills and
expertise are prerequisites.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.480
And if these sound like the kind of skills

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.790
you want to get better at, I
encourage you to take a look

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.890
at my Communication Coach Academy.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.190
We offer classes and group
coaching on communication skills,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.840
public speaking and leadership.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.180
There's a link in the
description below this video.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.710
Now let's talk about three ways

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.540
to come across like a leader.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.250
And if you think of some additional ways

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.120
to be seen as a leader,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.490
then please leave that in the comments

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.680
and we look forward to reading your tips.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.320
First, leaders solve problems.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.450
When the best leaders see a need,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.640
they take initiative, they take action.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.310
In contrast, many non-leader see a problem

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.950
and they're great about
talking about the problem,

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.140
maybe complaining, pointing the fingers

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.080
and expecting other people
to solve the problem.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:25.980
But the leader in the room

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.380
is the one who recommends
possible solutions

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.480
and offers to take action.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.350
And problem solvers have a
specific and learnable skillset.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.480
You have to be able to look at a problem,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.970
diagnose the possible
causes of that problem

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.710
and recommend a solution.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.530
As part of that, leaders must
be able to make decisions.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.050
That doesn't mean you need
to make decisions instantly

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.130
or without thought,

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.710
but I will say this, a
pattern of indecisiveness

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.960
won't demonstrate your leadership.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.880
So here's a tip within a tip,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.740
when you suggest solutions,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.630
be ready to break those solutions down

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.730
into a few action steps.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.400
A leader might say, for
example, "These are three steps

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.960
to solving this problem."

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.870
And then you would concisely
spell out those three steps,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.120
that demonstrates that
you thought carefully

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.470
about the solution.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.590
And by providing a solution
and a course of action,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.710
other people will literally
be following your plan

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.730
and that comes across

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.390
as an obvious problem solving
leadership moment for you.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:22.470
Other people notice that.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.810
So tip one, you'll come
across like a leader

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.850
if you solve problems.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.340
Second, leaders are good with groups.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.130
The best leaders make
contributions to group discussions

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.480
in three ways,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.890
effective leaders make contributions

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.320
on the task related work at meetings.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.230
So everything you say should
add value and be helpful

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.340
in one way or another.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.620
Specifically, most leaders stay on topic

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.090
and don't go on tangents.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.710
Leaders offer their point of view

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.890
or provide helpful information
on the task at hand

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.750
and if a conversation gets off track,

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.020
it's the leader's job to get
the conversation back on track.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.290
Now on this topic, I want
to offer a word of caution.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.090
Some people mistakenly believe

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.400
that the leader is the person
who should talk the most

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.750
at most meetings.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.210
that could be the case in some situations

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.670
if you're already the
official leader in the room.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.400
However, if there's already
an official leader there,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.440
you should not talk more than they do

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.720
and you shouldn't even be
the second place talker,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.553
we'll call it.

00:03:16.553 --> 00:03:18.750
If you were in a class in
college or high school,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.210
then you might remember how people respond

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.730
when one person talks
more than anyone else,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.410
it doesn't create a great vibe

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.380
for the other students or for the teacher

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.930
and you obviously don't
want to be the quietest

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.390
or most reserved person in the group.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.760
When it comes to task
related conversations,

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.590
you want to be right in the middle

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.810
and contribute about an average amount.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.850
Another way leaders
demonstrate their effectiveness

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.440
in groups is by excelling

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.290
at relationship oriented communication.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.990
Effective leaders take the
time to connect socially

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.180
with politeness and small talk

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.340
before the meeting officially kicks off.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.010
They don't isolate themselves
in the presence of the group,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.110
in other words, and
during the meeting itself,

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.830
they invest in relationships
by encouraging other people.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:05.310
Now, another word of caution
on this, don't overdo it.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.380
You don't want to focus so much on

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.890
how everybody's doing that
it becomes a preoccupation,

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.997
like, "Is everybody doing okay?"

00:04:12.997 --> 00:04:14.077
"Does anyone need anything?"

00:04:14.077 --> 00:04:16.920
"Is everybody comfortable
and feeling attended to?"

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.410
If you make taking care of other people

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.930
in the group your main focus,

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.540
you may be unintentionally
positioning yourself

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:28.890
as a merely supportive person
in the meeting, not a leader.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:29.723
The cure for this

00:04:29.723 --> 00:04:32.460
is to make plenty of task
related contributions

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.650
and be generally considerate of others,

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.650
but not preoccupied by
other people's needs.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.320
The next key way leaders
are good with groups

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.860
is they develop a skill of
facilitating the conversation.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.720
Here are some ways that you can do this.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.400
You can ask good questions,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.260
if there are quiet members in the group,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:50.880
but you can tell they
have something to say,

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.370
help them get into the
conversation a little bit.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.070
If the conversation is
too long or drifting,

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.930
you can refocus the discussion

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.710
by synthesizing the key themes
or main points you've heard

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.870
and then reflect that back to the group.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.076
You could say,

00:05:05.076 --> 00:05:07.980
for example, "It sounds like
we have three main concerns."

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.190
And then you would list those
three concerns concisely.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.390
Listening carefully

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.480
and crystallizing the discussion like this

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.970
is an incredibly valuable
group facilitation skill.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.520
So number two, you look
like a leader in the room

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.830
if you're good with groups.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.290
Third, leaders are
effective communicators,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.300
or at least most leaders are.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.070
And when leaders are at their best,

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.800
they're prepared, clear and concise

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.110
and if you do this, you'll
come across like a leader.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.090
When you give a status
update in front of the team,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.210
make sure you have your key
points sketched out beforehand.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.490
Talk through your outline a
few times before the meeting

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.100
to make sure you're crisp and on point.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.950
Another way leaders demonstrate
effective communication

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.230
is through the power of public speaking.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.080
When you're doing a standup
presentation for any reason,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.390
that's a leadership moment for you.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.520
Make the most of it by preparing days

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.000
or even weeks in advance if
it's an important presentation.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.340
And let's not forget good listening.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:05.250
There's an expression that
leaders are listeners.

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:08.093
You can show that you're a good
listener by paying attention

00:06:08.093 --> 00:06:10.530
and taking notes when other people speak.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.720
I had a supervisor for
years who took notes

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.420
during every single one-on-one
conversation we had.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.090
In contrast, if you were
to communicate well,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:19.920
when it's your turn to talk

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.960
and then look at your device or tune out

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.250
when other people are speaking,

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.320
you won't come across as a leader.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.240
You have to learn to listen carefully,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.210
even when the topic seems
to have nothing to do

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:32.400
with your area of expertise.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.200
In summary, if you want to come across

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.520
as the leader in the room,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.040
start working on solving problems,

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.900
get better at working in groups,

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.930
and work on your communication
skills at all levels.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:44.940
That's what the best leaders do.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.040
Knowing that, you can reverse engineer it

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.260
and help yourself come
across like a leader,

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.700
starting right away.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.110
And in that spirit, I wanted to tell you

